---
export const prerender = true;

import RoomLayout from '../../../layouts/RoomLayout.astro';
import ZybachNav from '../../../components/zybach/ZybachNav.astro';
import WorkingEditionBanner from '../../../components/zybach/WorkingEditionBanner.astro';
import {
  rights,
  rightsPopulations,
  derivedRights,
  rightsLabel,
} from '../../../components/zybach/_data';

/* The per-population statements are read from the collection's own rights
   file, so this page and the statement printed on an item page cannot drift
   apart. If the file has not landed, the page still renders and says so. */
const populations = rightsPopulations();
const derived = derivedRights();

const CONTACT = 'library@schooltrusts.net';
const CONTACT_MAILTO = `mailto:${CONTACT}?subject=${encodeURIComponent(
  'The Zybach Collection — a request',
)}`;
---

<RoomLayout
  room="collections"
  title="Rights, use, and takedown"
  subtitle="How the Library decides what it can publish, what it knows and does not know about the rights in this collection, and how to ask for a change."
  width="prose"
  sourceId="collections-zybach-rights"
  parents={[{ label: 'The Zybach Collection', href: '/collections/zybach/' }]}
>
  <span data-pagefind-meta="collection:zybach" hidden></span>

  <div class="source-page">
    <ZybachNav current="rights" />
    <WorkingEditionBanner />

    <h2 id="what-this-is">What this page is</h2>

    <p>
      This collection publishes oral history: recordings, transcripts and
      printed volumes in which people described their own lives, their
      families, their work and the places they lived. Some of the people who
      spoke are still living. Some died decades ago. Some of what they said is
      precise and generous. Some of it is wrong, and some of it is ugly.
    </p>

    <p>
      We publish it because it matters, and because a person's account of their
      own life belongs in the world. But publishing other people's words
      carries obligations, and this page sets out what ours are: how we decide
      what we can publish, how to tell us we have got something wrong, what we
      will do about it, and how fast.
    </p>

    <p>
      We would rather be told than not told. Nobody who writes to us about this
      material is treated as an adversary.
    </p>

    <h2 id="how-we-decide">How we decide what we can publish</h2>

    <p>
      Everything we put online rests on at least one of five bases, and we
      record which one for every group of material, so you can see our reasoning
      rather than take our word for it.
    </p>

    <ol class="zc-bases">
      <li>
        <strong>The work is in the public domain.</strong> Copyright has
        expired, or the work never carried copyright to begin with. Nothing in
        this collection currently rests on this basis.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We own the rights.</strong> The Library holds copyright, or
        holds permission broad enough to cover what we have done. This covers
        the indexes and apparatus we built ourselves, and nothing else.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We have the creator's participation, and are documenting his
        permission.</strong> Bob Zybach, who made this collection, reviewed this
        edition with the Library in August 2026 and publishes the same material
        himself. His written permission for this working edition is being
        recorded now, and the formal instrument follows; if it is not given,
        this edition comes down.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Our use is a fair use.</strong> For material still in copyright
        where we could not obtain permission, we publish under a considered
        fair-use rationale for education, scholarship and research. This is a
        judgment, not a certainty, and we treat it as one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We know of no restriction.</strong> After a documented search we
        found no evidence that anyone holds rights our use would infringe. "No
        known restrictions" means exactly that: no known ones. It is not a claim
        that none exist.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      <strong>Custody is not ownership.</strong> Holding a copy of something
      does not make you its owner. Some of what we describe is held by archives
      that have the physical originals but not the copyright, and we do not
      describe those institutions as having granted rights they do not have.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>We do not put a single rights label on the whole collection.</strong>
      This material was made at different times, under different agreements, by
      different people, for different institutions. Some of it was covered by a
      signed release; some by a different signed release with different terms;
      some, as far as anyone can now tell, by nothing at all. One label across
      all of it would be a convenient falsehood. Each group carries its own
      statement, below and on the item pages themselves.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-we-know">What we know about rights in this collection</h2>

    <p>
      Plainly, and including what we do not know. The short codes are from
      RightsStatements.org, the standard vocabulary libraries and archives use
      so that a statement means the same thing everywhere:
      <strong>In Copyright</strong> means the work is protected and we are
      telling you who to ask; <strong>Copyright Undetermined</strong> means we
      have looked and cannot say who owns it.
    </p>

    {populations.length === 0 && (
      <p class="zc-missing">
        The per-item rights statements are not available on this page right now.
        Write to us at <a href={CONTACT_MAILTO}>{CONTACT}</a> and we will send
        them.
      </p>
    )}

    {populations.map((p) => (
      <section class="zc-pop" aria-label={p.label}>
        <h3 class="zc-pop__head">
          {p.label}
          {p.dates && <span class="zc-pop__dates">{p.dates}</span>}
        </h3>
        <p class="zc-pop__status">{rightsLabel(p)}</p>
        {p.gloss && <p class="zc-pop__gloss">{p.gloss}</p>}
        {p.detail && <p class="zc-pop__detail">{p.detail}</p>}
        {p.disclosure && (
          <p class="zc-pop__disclosure">
            <strong>Money owed to somebody else.</strong> {p.disclosure}
          </p>
        )}
        {p.never && <p class="zc-pop__never">{p.never}</p>}
        {p.escalation && (
          <p class="zc-pop__detail">
            Where we have searched for a particular person and cannot find them,
            that one item is marked <strong>{p.escalation.statement}</strong>
            {p.escalation.code && <> ({p.escalation.code})</>}. {p.escalation.note}
          </p>
        )}
        {p.pointers && p.pointers.length > 0 && (
          <>
            <p class="zc-pop__ask">Who to ask:</p>
            <ul class="zc-pop__pointers">
              {p.pointers.map((ptr) => (
                <li>
                  {ptr.href ? <a href={ptr.href}>{ptr.label}</a> : ptr.label}
                  {ptr.note && <span class="zc-pop__note"> — {ptr.note}</span>}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </>
        )}
        {p.confirm && (
          <p class="zc-pop__open">
            <strong>Still open:</strong> {p.confirm}
          </p>
        )}
      </section>
    ))}

    {derived && (
      <section class="zc-pop zc-pop--derived" aria-label={derived.label}>
        <h3 class="zc-pop__head">
          {derived.label}
          {derived.dates && <span class="zc-pop__dates">{derived.dates}</span>}
        </h3>
        <p class="zc-pop__status">{derived.statement}</p>
        {derived.gloss && <p class="zc-pop__gloss">{derived.gloss}</p>}
        {derived.detail && <p class="zc-pop__detail">{derived.detail}</p>}
        {derived.built_with && <p class="zc-pop__detail">{derived.built_with}</p>}
      </section>
    )}

    <p>
      We keep a dated log of the rights work behind these statements — each step
      taken, each source consulted, each question asked and the answer we got,
      including the answers that were "no" and the ones that were "we don't
      know". Ask and we will send it. A rights statement that shows only its
      conclusions is asking you to trust us, and we would rather show the
      working.
    </p>

    <h2 id="narrators">If you are a narrator, or a narrator's family</h2>

    <p class="zc-standout">
      If you are one of the people whose interview appears here, or you are next
      of kin of someone who is, and you want something restricted or removed, we
      will do it. You do not have to give a reason and you do not have to have a
      legal claim.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is not a copyright question and we will not turn it into one.
      Whatever the paperwork says, whatever a release form signed thirty years
      ago allows us to do, a person who spoke into a microphone about their own
      life — or that person's family — gets to change their mind about what
      happens to it. There is a real professional disagreement about this. Some
      in our field would say a defensible legal position is enough. We do not
      think it is.
    </p>

    <p>What that means in practice:</p>

    <ul class="zc-practice">
      <li>
        Write to us and say what you want — removal, restriction, a note added,
        a name taken out, a passage cut. Whatever it is.
      </li>
      <li>
        We act on it. <strong>We do not investigate first, we do not ask you to
        prove anything, and we do not send you to a lawyer.</strong>
      </li>
      <li>
        If you want something taken out of our search results and indexes
        straight away while we sort out the rest, say so and we do that first.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you want an item gone entirely, it goes. We keep a preservation copy
        that nobody can see, unless you ask us not to, and we tell you which.
      </li>
      <li>We confirm in writing what we did.</li>
      <li>You can change your mind again later, in either direction.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      If you are not sure whether you count as next of kin for this purpose,
      write to us anyway. We would rather have that conversation than not.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sensitive">Sensitive information</h2>

    <p>
      Some material here is sensitive for reasons that have nothing to do with
      copyright, and we handle it separately.
    </p>

    <h3 id="site-locations">Places that should not be easy to find</h3>

    <p>
      These volumes contain passages in which narrators describe where American
      Indian burial, grave and artifact sites are. Turning that prose into a
      searchable, mappable point is not the same act as printing it on page 100
      of a monograph in 1993. It is our act, performed now, and it would make
      those places easy to find rather than merely findable.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>So those pages are held, and so are the index entries, place pages
      and timeline entries that would lead a reader to them.</strong> They stay
      held until we have consulted the Confederated Tribes of Grand Ronde and
      the Confederated Tribes of Siletz Indians, whose country this collection
      covers. That consultation is being opened now, and nothing locational
      will be published before it concludes.
      Silence is not agreement, and we will not read it as agreement. If a
      tribal government or tribal historic preservation office asks us to
      withhold, generalise or restrict anything else here, we do it on request
      and discuss it afterwards.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two things this does not mean. It does not mean we are withholding
      Kalapuya history — that history stays, and it is one of the reasons this
      collection is worth reading. And it does not mean we have deleted
      anything: a held page is still in the sequence, still citable, and says it
      is held.
    </p>

    <h3 id="personal-data">Personal information about living people</h3>

    <p>
      Pages carrying home addresses, telephone numbers, tax lots and the names
      of children are held, with the reason shown. Historical addresses that are
      part of the story — where a family farmed, which house burned — are
      treated as content rather than contact details, but tell us if we have got
      that balance wrong for you.
    </p>

    <h3 id="other-sensitive">Other sensitive content</h3>

    <p>
      The collection contains derogatory language, unproven allegations about
      named people, and references to children who are now adults. We do not
      quietly delete this material — altering the historical record is its own
      kind of dishonesty — but we note it, and there are several things short of
      deletion we can do. The list is further down this page. The
      <a href="/collections/zybach/content-note/">content note</a> says what is
      in this collection and where.
    </p>

    <h2 id="machines">Machine assistance, stated plainly</h2>

    <p>
      The apparatus around this collection was built with AI assistance under
      human direction: the page anchoring that lets you link to one printed
      page, the indexes, the place and people pages, and the timeline. That is
      the Library's layer, and it is labelled as the Library's layer wherever it
      appears.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>The transcripts are the creator's printed text, unaltered.</strong>
      They were typed, checked against the tape, and approved by the narrators
      before the volumes were printed. We extracted that text; we did not
      generate it, rewrite it, summarise it, correct it or enhance it. No
      generated text is presented as source material. No image has been
      sharpened, upscaled or interpolated. No audio has been synthesised and no
      narrator's voice has been re-rendered.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Library notifies Oregon State University's Special Collections and
      Archives Research Center, which holds the original recordings, of this use
      and of the machine assistance behind it, under the Center's notification-of-use
      practice.
    </p>

    <p>
      Machine alignment makes mistakes. If you find one, tell us — corrections
      are welcome and are not takedown requests. The
      <a href="/collections/zybach/errata/">errata page</a> logs what we have
      fixed, and the <a href="/collections/zybach/colophon/">colophon</a> says
      exactly what was done and what was deliberately not done.
    </p>

    <h2 id="asking">Asking us to change or remove something</h2>

    <p>Write to us. There is no form and no fee.</p>

    <p>Please include, as far as you can:</p>

    <ul class="zc-practice">
      <li>
        <strong>Your name, and how to reach you.</strong> We need to be able to
        reply.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Which page or pages</strong> — a link is ideal; the volume and
        printed page number is just as good.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your relationship to the material</strong>, if you are willing
        to say: narrator, family member, rights holder, someone named or
        described, a tribal government or office, an institution, or none of
        these. This is not required, but it changes how fast we act, and in some
        cases it decides the matter.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>What the problem is</strong> — copyright, privacy, cultural
        sensitivity, a factual error, harmful or offensive content, or something
        else.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>What you would like us to do.</strong> The list in the next
        section may help, but you do not have to choose from it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      A request missing some of this still gets a response. We will not bounce
      anything back on a technicality. If we need more, we will ask.
    </p>

    <h2 id="clock">What happens when we receive a request</h2>

    <ul class="zc-clock">
      <li>
        <strong>Within 2 business days</strong> — we acknowledge your request
        and tell you who is handling it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Immediately, on request</strong> — we take the material out of
        our search results, our indexes and our maps while we look into it. You
        only have to ask. Doing that is not a finding that you are right; it is
        what we do while we work out whether you are.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Immediately</strong> — if a tribal government or tribal historic
        preservation office asks for material to be withheld pending discussion,
        we withhold first and discuss afterwards.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Within 10 business days</strong> — we give you a decision in
        writing, with our reasons. If we cannot meet that, we tell you before
        the ten days are up, explain why, and give you a date.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Afterwards</strong> — if you think we got it wrong, say so and a
        different person reviews it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>Before we permanently remove anything, the person who gave us the
      material gets to be heard.</strong> We tell him what has been asked and
      why. That is not a veto — the decision is ours — but somebody who trusted
      us with a collection deserves to know before part of it disappears, and he
      often knows something we do not.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Two exceptions, and they matter.</strong> A request from a
      narrator or their next of kin is acted on at once and is not delayed for
      anyone's comment. And taking something out of the indexes happens straight
      away when you ask; the conversation happens while it is out, not before.
    </p>

    <p>We keep a record of every request, what was asked, what we did, and why.</p>

    <h2 id="responses">What we can do — the range of responses</h2>

    <p>
      We do not have only two settings. Removal is one option among many, and it
      is often not the one that fixes the problem. We start with the least
      drastic response that genuinely addresses what has been raised, and go
      further where that is what you want.
    </p>

    <ul class="zc-ladder">
      <li>
        <strong>Edit the description.</strong> Correct a name, a date, a place,
        a heading. The commonest fix and often the whole fix.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Add a note.</strong> The material stays as it is, with context
        attached — including context you write, if you want to write it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Anonymize.</strong> Replace a person's name with a description
        of their role, so the account survives without the identification.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Redact.</strong> Remove a specific passage, name or detail from
        the published page while keeping the rest available.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>De-index.</strong> The page stays at its own address but comes
        out of our search results, indexes and maps, so it is no longer put in
        front of people who were not looking for it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hold.</strong> The page is withdrawn from view but stays in the
        sequence, citable, saying that it is held.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Restrict.</strong> The material is available only on request, or
        only to a defined group — researchers, family members, a tribal office —
        on stated terms.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Remove.</strong> It comes down. We keep a preservation copy
        nobody can see unless we are asked not to, and we tell you which.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      We explain which of these we chose and why. If you asked for removal and
      we think de-indexing solves the problem, we will say so and ask what you
      think — and if you still want removal, that carries a great deal of weight
      with us.
    </p>

    <h2 id="will-not">What we will not do</h2>

    <ul class="zc-practice">
      <li>
        <strong>We will not charge you.</strong> There is no fee for a request,
        an appeal, or a copy of our record of what we did.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We will not treat a request as an accusation.</strong> Most of
        what we get told is a correction, and corrections make the collection
        better.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We will not remove something and pretend it was never
        there.</strong> A withdrawn page leaves a stub saying that something was
        removed, on what date, and in general terms why — without identifying
        anyone who asked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We will not hide behind the paperwork.</strong> "The release
        form allows it" is an answer to a legal question. It is not an answer to
        a narrator or a family, and we will not use it as one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We will not claim rights we do not have.</strong> We do not put
        an open licence on material we do not own, and we do not tell you our
        sources are freely reusable when we do not know that they are.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>America's School Trust Library</strong><br />
      an Oregon public benefit nonprofit corporation, Oregon Registry No.
      259005891
    </p>

    <p>
      Email <a href={CONTACT_MAILTO}>{CONTACT}</a>, with "The Zybach Collection"
      somewhere in the subject line so it reaches the right person.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every request gets a person, an acknowledgment within two business days,
      and a written answer with reasons. Nobody who writes to us about this
      material is treated as an adversary. Requests are handled by the
      Library's president; an appeal is reviewed by a Library director who took
      no part in the first decision.
    </p>

    <p class="zc-revised">
      This page was last revised on 4 September 2026. When it changes, the
      change is logged on the
      <a href="/collections/zybach/errata/">errata page</a>, so you can see what
      it said before.
    </p>

    <p class="see-also">
      <a href="/collections/zybach/">Finding aid</a> ·
      <a href="/collections/zybach/provenance/">Provenance</a> ·
      <a href="/collections/zybach/content-note/">Content note</a> ·
      <a href="/collections/zybach/colophon/">Colophon</a> ·
      <a href="/collections/zybach/errata/">Errata</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</RoomLayout>

